# THE HEADING IS THE PLACE'S NAME. Replace [Name of setting N] with the name as
# soon as you have it; the brackets only mark that it is unfilled. The
# classification does not go in the heading: it goes in the :Type: property,
# which is what org-scribe reads.
#
# Every setting is a level-1 heading. Do not nest them: org-scribe recognizes
# settings that way.
#
# The world as a whole — the four axes, how it presses on the protagonist's
# Weakness — lives in worldbuilding.org. This file holds the specific places
# where scenes happen.
#
# A setting with no Aspect at all is scenery. In revision Pass 5, settings used
# only as scenery are candidates for cutting.

* [Name of setting 1]
:PROPERTIES:
:Type: Setting
:Echo:
:First-appearance:
:END:

- Echo ::
  # How this place reflects the Theme. This is what makes it part of the
  # argument instead of a backdrop. "Everyone here speaks between the lines."
- Hazard ::
  # How this place makes the World Problem worse. "Everyone is looking for
  # someone to pin the blame on."
- Character ::
  # A character Aspect this place activates. "I owe money to half the people
  # in this room."
- Symbol ::
  # The object or detail that charges the scene. "The cup I never get to drink."

** Description

# What can be seen, heard and smelled. Only what a focalizer would notice while
# standing there.

